// The committed set. Fixture corpus only — no real prompt text, no real
// project, client or path appears in this file or in evals/fixture/.
//
// Every query is written the way somebody remembers a conversation weeks
// later: the thing it was *about*, not the words they typed at the time. The
// previous set failed that test — "webhook rate limited by the gateway"
// against a prompt saying "the outbound webhook is getting rate limited by
// their gateway" measures substring overlap, not retrieval, and scored 10/10
// with every answer at rank 1. `class` records what each query is for.
//
//   concept   the answer shares almost no content words with the query. bm25
//             can genuinely fail here; this is where the vector half earns its
//             350 ms.
//   ranking   the words appear, and so do they in three or four distractor
//             sessions on adjacent topics. Getting it right is ordering.
//   recall    the ordinary case: a couple of distinctive words remembered
//             correctly, competing against a corpus that shares them.
//
// `expected_sidechain` asserts the answer came back as the subagent
// transcript, not merely as some session whose id starts the same way — a
// claude sidechain id is `<parent>:agent-<hash>`, so a prefix test alone
// cannot tell the two apart.
{"query": "charged the customer twice because the client retried", "expected_session_prefix": "7c1d0e44", "expected_harness": "claude", "class": "concept", "note": "concept — the session never says charged, customer or twice"}
{"query": "combining keyword and vector search into one ranked list", "expected_session_prefix": "cbcfda7e", "expected_harness": "claude", "class": "recall", "note": "remembered by topic, not by phrase"}
